# Line endings. Written 15.08.2026, after the first Windows checkout of this
# repository cost an hour to a failure that named the wrong thing.
#
# What happened: the Git for Windows installer writes core.autocrlf=true into
# the SYSTEM gitconfig, so every text file in a fresh clone is materialised with
# CRLF. The Chromium tree does not get this treatment — gclient sets
# core.autocrlf=false for its own checkouts — so apply.sh ended up feeding CRLF
# patches to an LF tree, and `git apply` reported only:
#
#     !! 0001-fp-config-plumbing.patch DOES NOT APPLY
#
# which reads as "the patch is stale against this Chromium version" and sends
# you to rebase a patch that was perfectly correct.
#
# Telling contributors to fix their global config does not work: the setting is
# system-level, it is the installer's default, and it is re-applied by the next
# Git update. The repository has to state its own requirement.

# Patches are diffs against a tree that is LF. A CRLF patch does not apply, and
# -text means "never convert this, in either direction, on any platform".
*.patch -text

# Shell scripts run under Git Bash on Windows. A CRLF shebang makes bash report
# `/usr/bin/env^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory` — an error that
# names an interpreter which is installed and fine.
*.sh text eol=lf

# GN reads these on every platform and the build is byte-compared across
# machines for task 3.1; line endings must not vary by who checked out.
*.gn  text eol=lf
*.gni text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one place CRLF is the native convention.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
